-- title:
--   Planar local Poincaré inequality for pairs seeing neither cone direction
-- statement:
--   Work in the plane $\mathbb{R}^2$. Let $v_s, v_t$ be unit vectors with $\operatorname{cross}_2(v_s,v_t) = (v_s)_0(v_t)_1 - (v_s)_1(v_t)_0 \ne 0$, let $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, let $\alpha \ge 0$, and let $f : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}$ be measurable. Let $U, U' \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2$ be arbitrary sets, and let $P \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2 \times \mathbb{R}^2$ be a measurable set of pairs with
--
--   $$P \subseteq U \times U', \qquad \text{and for every } (s,t) \in P: \quad s \ne t, \quad t - s \notin V(v_s,\vartheta), \quad t - s \notin V(v_t,\vartheta),$$
--
--   where
--   $$V(w,\vartheta) \;=\; \tilde V(w,\vartheta) \cup \bigl(-\tilde V(w,\vartheta)\bigr), \qquad \tilde V(w,\vartheta) = \Bigl\{h \ne 0 : \cos\vartheta < \tfrac{\langle w, h\rangle}{\lVert h\rVert}\Bigr\},$$
--   is the double cone with axis $w$ and aperture $\vartheta$ (`doubleCone w ϑ`). Thus $P$ consists of *bad* pairs: distinct points whose difference lies in neither of the two double cones, so that neither endpoint sees the other along a cone direction. Then
--
--   $$\frac{\sin^4\vartheta}{4}\,\omega_2 \iint_{P} \big(f(t)-f(s)\big)^2\,\|s-t\|^{-2-\alpha}\,d(s,t) \;\le\; c\,\omega_2 \int_{U}\!\!\int_{\{z\,:\,z-s \in V(v_s,\vartheta)\}} 2\big(f(z)-f(s)\big)^2\|z-s\|^{-2-\alpha}\,dz\,ds \;+\; c\,\omega_2 \int_{U'}\!\!\int_{\{z\,:\,z-t \in V(v_t,\vartheta)\}} 2\big(f(t)-f(z)\big)^2\|z-t\|^{-2-\alpha}\,dz\,dt ,$$
--
--   where
--   $$c \;=\; \Bigl(\frac{1}{|\operatorname{cross}_2(v_s,v_t)|} + 1\Bigr)^{4+\alpha}\cdot\frac{4}{\sin^2\vartheta} \quad (\texttt{planarConst})$$
--   and $\omega_2 = |\bar B(0,1)|$ is the area of the unit disc (`unitBallVol 2`).
--
--   The inequality bounds the fractional energy carried by the pairs that see *neither* cone — the pairs on which the kernel's lower bound gives no information — by the two cone-restricted energies, one for each axis, over the two ambient sets. In dimension two this is what closes the gap: two non-parallel lines always meet, so a positive-measure set of intermediate points sees both directions, and the bad pairs can be chained through them.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** All quantities are in `ℝ≥0∞`, so the inequality carries content only when the right-hand side is finite. The factor $\omega_2$ appears on both sides and is not cancelled. The inner integrals on the right range over all $z \in \mathbb{R}^2$ in the translated cone, not merely over $U$ or $U'$. The sets $U$ and $U'$ are not assumed measurable; only $P$ is.
-- source:
